questions.detailed:
Fonts are render-affecting resources: until they arrive, the browser either hides text or shows a fallback.

## Effects
- **LCP**: when the LCP element is text, a blocked font delays its paint.
- **CLS**: a fallback font with different width or height shifts lines when the web font swaps in.
- **Network**: extra requests, sometimes from a third-party origin with its own connection setup.

## Strategy
| Technique | Why |
|---|---|
| WOFF2 format | Best compression for web fonts |
| Subsetting (e.g. only needed Unicode ranges) | Smaller files; split by script for global sites |
| Fewer families, weights and styles; variable fonts where they replace many files | Fewer requests and bytes |
| Self-host or serve from your CDN | Avoid third-party connection costs |
| Preload only the critical above-the-fold font | Early discovery without crowding other resources |
| `font-display` | Control behaviour while loading |
| Metric-matched fallback | Reduce shift on swap |

`font-display` options in short: `swap` shows fallback immediately then swaps (risk of shift); `optional` uses the font only if it is available very quickly (least shift, may not use the web font on first visit); `fallback` is a compromise.

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: "Brand Sans";
  src: url("/fonts/brand-sans-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-display: swap;
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Brand Sans Fallback";
  src: local("Arial");
  size-adjust: 104%;
  ascent-override: 92%;
  descent-override: 24%;
}
body { font-family: "Brand Sans", "Brand Sans Fallback", sans-serif; }
```

(The override numbers above are an example; compute real values for your font.)

## Common mistakes
- Preloading every font file.
- Preloading a font without `crossorigin`, so it is fetched twice.
- Shipping many weights that are rarely used.
